course: medicine
lesson: 59
title: Changing-room germs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A changing room is warm, wet and full of bare feet, sweaty kit and shared stuff, which is exactly what viruses, fungi and bacteria love, so most sport germs travel by floors, skin, towels and bottle tops.
explain: Lucas walks barefoot across the wet floor every week and ends up with verrucas on three toes, «Bro, how is this even fair?»; a pair of flip-flops would have kept his feet off the floor where the verruca virus waits.
explain: Getting one of these doesn't mean you're dirty: Freya's impetigo after judo and Kyle's «ringworm», which is a fungus and not a worm at all, come from close contact on shared mats.
explain: The rules are short: your own bottle, your own towel, your own gum shield, flip-flops in the showers, kit out of the bag and washed after every session, and tell an adult about any new rash or sore.
fact: A verruca is a wart on the foot caused by a virus; the NHS says you can still swim if you cover it with a waterproof plaster or a verruca sock.
fact: The black dots in a verruca are tiny blood vessels, not roots, and cutting or picking it can hurt and spread it.
fact: The verruca virus is often picked up from warm wet floors where people walk barefoot, so flip-flops or pool shoes help.
fact: Athlete's foot is a fungus that likes warm damp skin between the toes; anyone can get it, and a pharmacy cream and dry feet usually clear it.
fact: Spit on a shared bottle can carry cold viruses, cold sores and stomach bugs, and wiping the top on a shirt doesn't remove them.
fact: Cold sores are caused by a virus spread by close skin contact, so doctors advise against contact sport while a sore is open.
fact: Impetigo is a skin infection spread by touch and common in mat sports, with sores that dry into golden crusts; the NHS says stay off school until the sores crust over or 48 hours after starting antibiotics.
fact: Ringworm is a fungus, not a worm, that makes a ring-shaped rash and spreads by skin contact, shared mats and towels; antifungal cream usually treats it.
fact: Fresh sweat barely smells; the stink comes from bacteria feeding on sweat in a warm closed bag.
fact: Damp shared towels can pass on athlete's foot, verrucas and skin infections, and washing towels at 60°C gets rid of more germs.
fact: A gum shield should be rinsed in cold water and kept in its case; sugary drinks feed germs, and hot water can bend it out of shape.
fact: «Lekker» is South African for great, «now now» means very soon, «cooldrink» is a fizzy drink, and «rank» is British slang for disgusting.
exercise: Aoife from Galway has a verruca. Can she go swimming? | Yes, with it covered by a waterproof plaster | No, never until it's gone | Yes, but only with the verruca bare | No, verrucas spread through the air
exercise: What are the black dots in a verruca? | Tiny blood vessels | Roots growing into the foot | Little worms | Dirt that won't wash out
exercise: Where did Lucas most likely pick up his verrucas? | The wet changing-room floor | Touching his own hands after school | Drinking a mouthful of pool water | Wearing his own socks for too long
exercise: What would have kept Lucas's feet safe? | Flip-flops or pool shoes | Walking faster across the floor | Jumping in the pool sooner | Wearing wet socks
exercise: Josh's toes itch in wet footy socks. What is athlete's foot? | A fungus that loves damp skin | A virus that lives inside footballs | A tiny worm that lives in the sock | A bruise from boots that are too tight
exercise: How many gaps between the toes on two feet need drying? | 8 | 4 | 10 | 5
exercise: Aiden wipes the bottle on his shirt before sharing. Does that make it clean? | No, a shirt doesn't remove germs | Yes, any kind of wiping kills all germs | Yes, if the shirt is sweaty | No, but sharing is still fine
exercise: Lwazi has a cold sore but wants to scrum. Why does the coach bench him? | It can spread by skin contact | Cold sores make you slow | Benches are for cold sores | It's only catching by kissing
exercise: Freya gets crusty golden sores after judo. What is it and does it mean she's dirty? | Impetigo, and no, anyone can catch it | Impetigo, and yes, she's dirty | Sunburn, from the gym lights | Chickenpox, from the mats
exercise: When can Freya go back on the mat after starting antibiotics? | After 48 hours, or when sores crust over | The next morning | After 48 minutes | Only after a whole year
exercise: Kyle: «Ringworm?! There's a worm in me?!» What's true? | It's a fungus, not a worm | It's a worm under the skin | It's a bite from a mat | It's a burn from the rope
exercise: Why does Leo's PE bag stink by Friday? | Bacteria fed on sweat for days | Sweat smells awful straight away | Kit goes off like milk | The bag itself is rotting
exercise: Hannah borrows Molly's damp towel. What could it pass on? | Athlete's foot, verrucas or skin infections | Nothing, towels are always clean | Only a cold in the nose | A broken bone
exercise: How should Bongani clean his gum shield? | Rinse in cold water, keep in its case | Rinse it in fizzy drink to freshen it up | Boil it in a pan of hot water every night | Lend it to a mate to dry out in his bag
exercise: Which is a good changing-room rule? | Your own bottle, towel and gum shield | Share everything to be a team player | Bare feet everywhere is fine if the floor looks dry | Keep wet kit in the bag all week
test: Aoife: «Mam says I can't go to swimming, but sure it's grand, isn't it?» Who's closer to right? | Aoife, if she covers the verruca | Mam, because verrucas mean no pool ever | Neither, she must cut it out | Mam, the pool will close
test: Lucas's friend says: «Just dig the roots out with a pin». What would happen? | It could hurt and spread the verruca | It would pull out the roots and cure it | It would cure it at once | Nothing, verrucas feel no pain
test: Josh from Adelaide: «Itchy as, mate» («itchy as» means very itchy). What should his mum suggest? | Dry between the toes and use pharmacy cream | Keep the wet socks on longer | Rub mud on the toes | Share socks with his brother
test: Half of Aiden's team drinks from one bottle. What would happen if one player had a stomach bug? | Several teammates could catch it | The bug would die as soon as it hit the bottle | Only the very last player to drink gets ill | Wiping the top on a shirt stops it spreading
test: Lwazi: «It's just a small one, it'll be lekker». What does the doctor's advice say? | No contact sport while the sore is open | Small sores are fine in the scrum if you're careful | Play, but only in the second half | Cover it with mud and play
test: Kyle's sister teases: «Ringworm means you're manky». What's the truth? | It's a common fungus from shared mats | Only dirty people who never wash get ringworm | It's caught from earthworms | It means he has a parasite
test: Leo's sister screams «That's rank! Bin it!» What does «rank» mean? | Disgusting | Brilliant | Expensive | Wet
test: Hannah: «It's only a towel, what's the big deal?» Best reply? | Damp shared towels carry skin germs | Towels can't hold any germs once they're dry | Only new towels carry germs | Molly's towel is cleaner than hers
test: Bongani: «Now now it's clean, lekker», after rinsing in his fizzy cooldrink. What's wrong? | Sugar feeds germs, and sharing spreads them | Fizzy drinks kill all germs | Nothing, it's perfectly clean | He should use orange juice instead
test: A cartoon gym has a sign: «Sweat stinks, so never sweat». Correct it. | Bacteria on old sweat make the smell | Sweat itself always stinks the moment it appears | Sweating is bad for you and should be stopped | Only adults' sweat smells, children's never does
test: What would happen if Leo took his kit out and washed it after every session? | His bag wouldn't stink by Friday | It would smell even worse from all the washing | It would shrink to nothing in a couple of weeks | Bacteria would grow faster on the clean damp kit
exam: Identify the organism responsible for the condition commonly termed ringworm. | A fungus | A worm | A virus of the foot | An insect
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the guidance pertaining to swimming with a verruca? | Swimming is permitted provided the verruca is covered | Swimming is prohibited until the verruca is excised | Swimming is permitted only with the verruca exposed | Swimming is prohibited for a period of one year
exam: Ascertain the principal source of the odour of kit retained unwashed for several days. | Bacteria feeding upon perspiration | Perspiration in its fresh state | The fabric of the bag, which decays over time | Excessive washing of the garments
exam: It is given that a participant has an open cold sore. Select the appropriate course of action in respect of contact sport. | Abstention until the sore has healed | Participation in the scrum without restriction | Concealment of the sore with mud | Participation in the second half only
